;
; read_serve.pio -- PIO-driven read-trigger response, replacing the
; software poll loop in monitor.c's monitor_run() for reads only (writes
; are handled separately, see monitor.c's own comment on why).
;
; Design credit: the two-state-machine-plus-DMA-chain technique (one SM
; captures the live address into a ready-to-use absolute RAM pointer; a
; DMA chain uses that pointer to fetch the byte and hand it to a second
; SM that drives it onto the data pins when the trigger asserts) is
; adapted from Piers Finlayson's OneROM project
; (https://github.com/piersfinlayson/one-rom, firmware/src/piodma/piorom2.c),
; MIT licensed:
;
;   Copyright (c) 2026 Piers Finlayson
;
;   Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a
;   copy of this software and associated documentation files (the
;   "Software"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including
;   without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish,
;   distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to
;   permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to
;   the following conditions:
;
;   The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included
;   in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.
;
;   THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS
;   OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF
;   MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT.
;   IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY
;   CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT,
;   TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE
;   SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.
;
; The actual instruction sequences below are this project's own
; (adapted for this board's simpler, GreenPAK-pre-combined single-bit
; trigger lines -- no CS/BYTE combining logic needed, unlike OneROM's own
; general-purpose chip-agnostic design), not copied verbatim -- see this
; project's own board_pins.h for the real GPIO assignments the C setup
; code binds these programs to.
;
; 2026-09-22 HISTORY NOTE: this file went through two same-day detours
; and back. First, an OneROM-style continuous/unconditional capture
; redesign (3 SMs, both DMA channels dreq-paced continuously) made real-
; hardware reliability measurably worse across two attempts, reverted.
; Then a kyo-ta04 Pico2ROMEmu-inspired redesign (single SM, first still
; DMA-chained, then fully CPU-serviced with no DMA at all) was tried to
; chase STAGE DEBUG's long-standing "corrupts within ~150 bytes"
; reliability problem -- neither variant fixed it, and the single-SM
; version broke basic functionality outright (CALL hangs, keywords not
; recognized) before being fixed enough to test cleanly. That whole
; detour turned out to be chasing the wrong layer: a real-hardware
; diagnostic (RP2350 firmware serving a hardcoded 0xAA for every read,
; buffer[] lookup entirely disabled, isolating the mechanism from any
; data-content question) proved the corruption was electrical, not
; software -- traced to U6 (TXS0108EPWR), a known real limitation of that
; specific level-shifter's edge-rate-acceleration one-shot circuits under
; frequent bus-direction reversal (see
; PC1500-RP2350B-BLE/no_level_shifters_investigation.md's own 2026-09-22
; update for the full writeup and sources). U6 has since been physically
; removed (board owner's own hardware change), and this file reverts to
; the design below -- the best-performing version prior to that whole
; detour -- as the baseline to retest against a bus no longer fighting
; the level shifter's own retrigger behavior. Kept as git history, not
; repeated in full here.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; addr_capture (2026-09-18, edge-gated redesign -- fixes a real-hardware
; bug found over two nights of debugging: PEEK on the real PC-1500
; returned garbage/zero regardless of address). Root cause: the LH5801's
; 13-bit address bus is shared across the WHOLE machine's memory map, not
; exclusive to this board's own 8K window. The earlier version of this
; program captured and autopushed the address EVERY pass, unconditionally,
; at full (undivided) clock speed -- so it picked up the CPU's own
; unrelated ROM/RAM fetches elsewhere just as eagerly as genuine accesses
; to this window, and did so fast enough to permanently fill the
; downstream DMA relay + read_serve's 4-deep TX FIFO with addresses
; unrelated to whatever a real read trigger was about to serve (confirmed
; via real-hardware diagnostic: RXSTALL/TXOVER always set, addr_rxlvl/
; serve_txlvl always 4/4).
;
; Fixed by gating capture on PIN_TRIG_RD itself via JMP PIN (confirmed
; against the RP2040 datasheet section 3.4.2.2: "JMP PIN branches on the
; GPIO selected by EXECCTRL_JMP_PIN... The branch is taken if the GPIO is
; high" -- a separate, independently-configured input from this SM's own
; IN_BASE pin mapping below; see monitor.c's SetupReadServePio() for the
; matching sm_config_set_jmp_pin() call). While the trigger is low,
; nothing is captured or pushed to the RX FIFO at all. On the rising
; edge, the address is captured and autopushed EXACTLY ONCE, then this SM
; spins (without capturing again) for as long as the trigger stays high,
; only resuming the poll-for-rising-edge loop once the trigger goes low.
; This "conditional publishing" both (a) guarantees only genuine accesses
; to this window are ever captured, since a foreign access elsewhere on
; the shared bus never asserts this board's own trigger, and (b) keeps
; production and consumption matched 1:1 per access, so a stale entry
; from a previous, different-address access can never sit ahead of the
; current access's own entry in the 4-deep queue.
;
; Rationale for why "react after the trigger" is fast enough on THIS
; hardware, despite an earlier RP2350 *software* read-trigger path
; failing under real bus-trigger load (see commit d999fa0, "a still-
; unresolved probabilistic read-corruption issue under real bus-trigger
; load"): that failure was jitter (cache misses, dual-core bus
; contention, WiFi/BT background polling on this "W" board variant), not
; a fundamental timing impossibility -- the original PSoC5 firmware
; (Design01_NonDMA_8K_PV_Swap.cydsn/main.c lines 1304-1341) has run this
; exact "react to CS, then read the live address, then drive data" algorithm
; in plain software on real PC-1500 hardware for years. PIO removes the
; jitter (fixed-cycle-count instructions, dedicated GPIO synchronizer,
; isolated from flash cache/other-core activity) that broke the software
; version, making the same algorithm reliable when run on a state machine.
;
; IRQ handshake added (2026-09-18, round 2) -- real-hardware testing of
; the edge-gated design above found it dramatically better than before
; (many clean ECVER/PEEK cycles, versus never-working before) but not
; perfect: an eventual crash landed the LH5801 executing inside unrelated
; ROM code (SD_PARSE_QUOTED_UNTERMINATED, nowhere ECVER's own code path
; ever legitimately goes), the signature of a wrong byte having been
; served during a real access somewhere earlier and corrupting a computed
; jump. Root cause: this program and read_serve below were each
; independently executing their own `wait ... gpio 21` against the SAME
; raw, physically-async trigger pin -- two independent observers of one
; signal, with no guarantee they always agree on exactly how many edges
; occurred, especially since this program takes 2 extra instructions
; (the actual capture) between noticing the rising edge and getting back
; to checking for the falling edge, while read_serve gets there almost
; immediately. A glitch or hazard landing in that gap could make the two
; state machines disagree on access count, breaking the 1:1 production/
; consumption matching the whole design depends on.
;
; Fixed by making this program the SOLE observer of the raw pin, and
; handing already-synchronized "access started"/"access ended" events to
; read_serve via PIO's IRQ flags instead of letting it independently
; re-sample the pin at all (`irq nowait N` sets flag N without blocking;
; `wait 1 irq N` on the far side blocks until set, then consumes/clears
; it -- the standard inter-state-machine handshake idiom). With only one
; state machine ever reading PIN_TRIG_RD's raw level, there is no longer
; a second, independent opinion for it to disagree with.
.program addr_capture
.wrap_target
wait_high:
    jmp pin, got_it      ; PIN_TRIG_RD (mapped via sm_config_set_jmp_pin,
                         ; NOT the IN_BASE mapping below) sampled HIGH ->
                         ; branch and capture now
    jmp wait_high        ; still LOW -> keep polling; nothing captured or
                         ; pushed while the trigger is deasserted
got_it:
    in x, 19             ; shift in the fixed buffer-base prefix (32 - 13)
    in pins, 13          ; shift in the live 13-bit address bus; ISR now
                         ; holds buffer_base | address. Autopush (32-bit
                         ; threshold) fires HERE -- exactly one push per
                         ; rising edge of the trigger.
    irq nowait 0         ; tell read_serve: a fresh byte is on its way --
                         ; safe to start driving once it arrives
wait_low:
    jmp pin, wait_low    ; trigger still HIGH -> keep spinning here (NOT
                         ; capturing again) until it deasserts
    irq nowait 1         ; only reached once the jmp above falls through,
                         ; i.e. exactly when the trigger has gone LOW --
                         ; tell read_serve: access over, release the bus
.wrap

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; read_serve -- the actual bus-facing state machine. Data pins are inputs
; (Hi-Z) except for the brief window the read trigger is asserted, during
; which they're driven from whatever byte the DMA chain fed to this SM's
; own TX FIFO (fed continuously in the background by addr_capture's own
; output -- see monitor.c's DMA setup -- so the right byte for the
; *current* address is normally already waiting by the time the trigger
; asserts, not fetched fresh in response to it). Configured for autopull
; (8-bit threshold) so `out pins, 8` consumes a fresh byte from the TX
; FIFO with no separate `pull` instruction needed in the critical path.
;
; Instruction order (2026-09-18): `out pins, 8` executes BEFORE `mov
; pindirs, ~null` enables the output drivers, not after -- applying the
; same principle already fixed in monitor.c's DriveData() on 2026-09-17
; ("Value written BEFORE enabling output... avoids a stale-data window").
; Confirmed safe against the RP2040 datasheet section 3.5.6 "GPIO
; Mapping": PIO keeps a separate 32-bit register for each GPIO's output
; LEVEL and another for its output ENABLE (pindirs); a state machine can
; write either independently of the other. So `out pins, 8` safely
; latches the correct byte into the level register while pindirs is
; still input (Hi-Z), with no effect yet on the actual pad -- and by the
; time `mov pindirs, ~null` switches the pins to output on the very next
; instruction, that correct byte is already sitting there: the pins
; carry the right value from the first instant they go live, exactly
; like DriveData()'s own fix. This also means that if the DMA-fed byte
; for the CURRENT address isn't ready yet, `out pins, 8` (autopull)
; simply stalls here -- with the bus still Hi-Z, never driven with a
; stale value -- until it arrives; the same synchronous-wait behavior
; already validated by the PSoC5 precedent above.
;
; IRQ handshake (2026-09-18, round 2): this program no longer reads
; PIN_TRIG_RD's raw level itself at all -- see addr_capture's own header
; comment above for why two independent observers of the same async pin
; was a real bug, not just theoretical. `wait 1 irq 0`/`wait 1 irq 1`
; instead block on addr_capture's own already-synchronized "started"/
; "ended" events, so there is only ever one opinion about how many
; accesses have occurred.
.program read_serve
.wrap_target
    mov pindirs, null    ; release the data bus (inputs)
    wait 1 irq 0         ; block until addr_capture signals a fresh byte
                         ; is on its way (see its own header comment) --
                         ; NOT a raw PIN_TRIG_RD read any more
    out pins, 8          ; latch the pre-staged byte into the pin output
                         ; register NOW, while pins are still inputs --
                         ; see the header comment above for why this is
                         ; safe and correct
    mov pindirs, ~null   ; NOW drive the data bus (outputs) -- the byte is
                         ; already correctly latched, so no stale-data
                         ; window
    wait 1 irq 1         ; block until addr_capture signals the access
                         ; has ended (i.e. PIN_TRIG_RD has gone low)
.wrap
